| Hardware / Device Component | Description |
|-----------------------------|-------------|
| Smartphone (Android / iOS) | A mobile phone with a full operating system capable of running corporate applications. Key specifications to assess: maker/brand/model, RAM capacity, storage size, screen display type, input method (touchscreen), connectivity (Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, NFC, cellular), OS version, and IMEI. The device must meet the minimum hardware and software requirements specified in the job order for all required applications. |
| Tablet (Android / iOS / Windows) | A larger-screen mobile device used for field work, meetings, presentations, or kiosk applications. Configuration is similar to a smartphone but may include stylus input, keyboard accessories, and larger storage for field data. Cellular-enabled tablets require a SIM card or eSIM for mobile data. Some organisations use tablets with rugged cases for outdoor or industrial use. |
| Laptop (portable computer) | A portable computer running Windows, macOS, or Linux. Configuration includes OS setup, network (Wi-Fi and wired), VPN, email, applications, and security (BitLocker or FileVault encryption, screen lock). Asset tagging and user handover documentation are part of commissioning. Laptops bridge the gap between desktop and mobile; they follow both desktop build and mobile device configuration procedures. |
| Connectivity interfaces | The connection types available on mobile devices: **Wi-Fi** (802.11ac/ax for wireless LAN), **Cellular** (4G/5G for mobile data), **Bluetooth** (short-range for peripherals like headsets, keyboards), **NFC** (near-field communication for contactless pairing or payment), **Infrared** (legacy, rare on modern devices), and **USB** (data transfer and charging). Each must be configured or disabled per the organisational security policy. |
| Mobile device security features | Built-in security capabilities: **screen lock** (PIN, pattern, fingerprint, face recognition), **device encryption** (full-disk encryption enabled by default on modern devices), **remote wipe** (erase device data remotely if lost or stolen, via MDM or Find My Device), **certificate store** (for corporate Wi-Fi 802.1X and VPN authentication), and **app permissions** (control which apps can access camera, microphone, location, contacts). |


| Software / Configuration Area | Description |
|-------------------------------|-------------|
| Network and connectivity configuration | Setting up Wi-Fi (SSID, security key, 802.1X if enterprise), cellular APN, Bluetooth pairing, and VPN client. IP addressing is typically DHCP for mobile devices. Authentication may use username/password, certificate, or both. Test each connection type after configuration: Wi-Fi signal strength, cellular data speed, VPN tunnel establishment, and Bluetooth peripheral pairing. |
| Email and collaboration setup | Configuring corporate email using Exchange ActiveSync, IMAP, or POP as specified in the job order. Settings include server address, port, SSL/TLS, username, and password or OAuth. Calendar and contacts sync are configured alongside email. Collaboration apps (e.g. Microsoft Teams, Google Workspace) are installed and signed in. Test by sending and receiving a test email and verifying calendar sync. |
| Mobile application management | Installing, configuring, and updating required applications per the job order. Apps may be deployed through an enterprise app store, MDM push, or manual installation. Verify software licences, version compatibility with the device OS, and minimum device requirements (RAM, storage). Configure app-specific settings (e.g. default browser, offline mode for field apps). Remove or restrict personal apps if required by company policy. |
| Security policy enforcement | Applying the organisational security policy to the device: enable device encryption, set screen lock timeout, enforce password complexity, enable remote wipe capability, restrict USB debugging, disable installation from unknown sources, and configure automatic OS and app updates. On MDM-managed devices, these policies are pushed automatically. On manually configured devices, each setting must be applied and documented individually. |

